<h1>Python for .NET Engineers</h1>
<p class="sub">Reading, writing and shipping Python when C# is your first language.</p>
<p class="sub-pl">Python dla inżynierów .NET — czytanie, pisanie i wdrażanie Pythona, gdy C# jest twoim pierwszym językiem.</p>

<ul class="pills">
  <li>{{chapters.written}} of {{chapters.total}} chapters written</li>
  <li>{{exercises}} exercises</li>
  <li>{{listings}} runnable listing files</li>
  <li>{{diagrams}} diagrams per edition</li>
  <li>English and Polish</li>
  <li>Free</li>
</ul>

<h2>Read it</h2>
<p>
  Both editions are built from one source and republished on every push, so
  the PDFs below are the current state of the repository rather than a
  snapshot somebody remembered to upload. They are built for a screen: A4,
  single-sided, with every listing and exercise sized to sit in a PDF viewer
  on the left of an editor on the right.
</p>
<div class="downloads">
  <a href="Python-for-dotNET-Engineers.pdf"><strong>English edition</strong><span>Python for .NET Engineers — PDF</span></a>
  <a href="Python-dla-inzynierow-dotNET.pdf"><strong>Wydanie polskie</strong><span>Python dla inżynierów .NET — PDF</span></a>
</div>

<div class="banner">
  <strong>In progress.</strong> {{chapters.written}} of the {{chapters.total}}
  chapters are written in both languages, along with
  {{appendices.written}} of {{appendices.total}} appendices; what is left is
  marked <em>planned</em> in the contents below and prints as a brief where
  the chapter will go. This paragraph's numbers are computed from the tree on
  every build, not typed, so the page cannot claim a chapter that is not
  there — or go on calling itself a scaffold after the book has been written.
</div>

<h2>Why</h2>
<p>
  A C# engineer who starts writing Python does not lack a language course.
  They have a mental model that is mostly right and wrong in a few specific
  places, and the places are where the incidents come from: a coroutine that
  is cold where a <code>Task</code> is hot, a type hint the runtime never
  checks, an <code>__eq__</code> that quietly makes an object unhashable, an
  environment that is a directory rather than a machine setting.
</p>
<p>
  So this is not a Python-from-zero course, not a library catalogue and not
  another AI book. It is a <strong>translation dictionary from C# to
  Python</strong> plus a <strong>field manual for shipping Python to
  production</strong>. Each chapter maps one thing the reader already knows
  onto its Python counterpart, elicits the habit that breaks, and ends with
  exercises whose tests run in CI on the pinned versions.
</p>

<h2>What is checked, and what that is worth</h2>
<p>
  A book about a toolchain goes stale the moment the toolchain moves, and a
  listing nobody ran is a guess with syntax highlighting. So the claims on
  the page are wired to the build:
</p>
<ul class="plain">
  <li>Every listing is a file under <code>code/</code> that CI executes on
    every push, against pinned versions, on the Python the book names.</li>
  <li>Every exercise ships as three files — a starter that <em>must fail</em>
    its test, a solution that must pass, and the test itself. CI runs both
    halves, so an exercise that has stopped being an exercise fails the
    build.</li>
  <li>Every measured number is produced by a script, committed, and compared
    against a fresh run; a figure that has drifted from the code that
    computed it stops the build rather than reaching a reader.</li>
  <li>Anything that could not be run carries a marker saying so, and the
    markers are counted on every build — the count stands at
    {{verifybox}} per edition today. A block nobody ran is a promise to the
    reader that it was not run, rather than a silence.</li>
  <li>Both editions are compared by a parity tool — structure, labels,
    figures, exercises and numeric literals, in order — so a correction to
    one language cannot quietly skip the other.</li>
</ul>

