<h2>The guiding project</h2>
<p>
  <code>trace-assert</code>: a Python port of the first layer of
  <a href="https://github.com/konradcinkusz/agent-eval-bench">agent-eval-bench</a>,
  deterministic assertions over an execution trace, in pytest. Chapter&nbsp;11
  introduces the trace model and the first two assertions, Chapter&nbsp;13
  records a model call into the same trace, and Chapter&nbsp;14 carries the
  rest of the set, the packaging, and a comparison with the same instrument's
  .NET and TypeScript ports. It is complete, it lives under
  <code>code/src/trace_assert/</code>, and the book's own CI has been running
  it since before the first chapter was written.
</p>

<h2>Where it sits</h2>
<p>
  It is volume zero of a trilogy. The
  <a href="https://github.com/konradcinkusz/llm-book">LangChain, LangGraph and
  Async Python</a> book is written for the engineer arriving from .NET and
  assumes the language and the toolchain; this book supplies them and stops
  where that one starts. Agent frameworks stay there, and
  <a href="https://github.com/konradcinkusz/maf-book">Microsoft Agent Framework
  for .NET Engineers</a> covers the .NET side. Where a topic is owned by one
  of the other two, this book points there in one sentence and does not
  repeat a section.
</p>

<h2>Releases</h2>
<div class="scroll">
<table>
  <tr><th scope="row">v0.1</th><td>Parts I and II — the runtime, the toolchain and the language.</td></tr>
  <tr><th scope="row">v0.2</th><td>Parts III and IV — concurrency and shipping.</td></tr>
  <tr><th scope="row">v1.0</th><td>Part V and the appendices.</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<p>
  Every chapter is written to stand on its own, under three thousand words of
  prose, and is published as soon as it is finished rather than held for the
  volume it belongs to.
</p>

